{
  "result": "There's a specific kind of quiet that only exists at 5:47 in the morning.\n\nIt's not night anymore, but it's not really day either. The sky is that washed-out blue-grey, like someone forgot to finish painting it. The streetlights are still on, but they look embarrassed about it. A single bird is trying out a song and hasn't decided if it means it yet.\n\nI love that time. Not because it's peaceful - although it is - but because it's a threshold.\n\nWe think life happens in the big, solid places - the house, the job, the relationship, the city. But actually, I think most of life happens in the thresholds. In the hallways. In the airports. In the text that says \"I'm on my way.\" In the moment you have your hand on the doorknob but haven't opened the door yet.\n\nThink about it.\n\nNo one writes songs about being *in* love as much as they write songs about *falling* in love. Or almost being in love. Or just falling out of it. That in-between hum is where all the electricity lives.\n\nMy favorite thresholds:\n\n**The library just before it closes.** The lights are still warm, but the librarians are starting to push in chairs. Everyone left inside is concentrated, reading a little faster, as if they could take the whole building home with them.\n\n**The moment an old song comes on in a grocery store.** For three minutes you're not an adult buying dish soap. You're 17 again, in someone's car, and summer is endless. Then the song fades and you're back by the avocados, slightly disoriented, slightly glowing.\n\n**When someone is about to tell you something honest and they take a breath first.** That little inhale is a whole universe. You can see them decide to be brave.\n\nWe spend so much time trying to get *somewhere*. To the weekend, to graduation, to being understood, to being healed. We treat the in-between as just waiting. As wasted time.\n\nBut the in-between is the only place that is truly alive. It's unfinished. It still has potential.\n\nA seed is a threshold between a flower and nothing. A doorway is a threshold between who you were and who you're about to be when you walk through it.\n\nMaybe that's why we feel so nostalgic for moments even while we're living them. Part of us knows we're standing on a threshold that will be gone in a second. We're trying to memorize a place we haven't even left yet.\n\nSo if you're in an in-between right now - not quite where you were, not quite where you're going - don't rush it.\n\nMake some tea. Notice the light. Listen to that one bird practicing its song.\n\nYou are exactly where the magic is.",
